Changes code from Helium's helium/core/noise/hardware-concurrency.patch (GPL-3.0, The Helium Authors).

What a page reads about the processor and the GPU is a common value instead of this machine's, so it adds little to a
fingerprint. Bits are from EFF's Cover Your Tracks (2026-11-20, about 367,000 browsers tested in the last 45 days).

- navigator.hardwareConcurrency, in pages, workers and a detached frame's navigator, is 8 on a machine with 8 or more
  cores, 4 with 4 to 7, and 2 below, instead of Helium's per-site pick of n, n-2 or n-4 from n clamped to 16: 2.0 bits
  for 8, against 4.4 to 7.2 for 12, 14 or 16. It is bucketed before a DevTools override applies, so emulation reports
  the value it sets.
- UNMASKED_RENDERER_WEBGL (WEBGL_debug_renderer_info), and the GL_RENDERER of a webglcontextcreationerror's message,
  name a common chip of the same Apple GPU generation instead of this one: "ANGLE (Apple, ANGLE Metal Renderer: Apple
  M1, Unspecified Version)" for every M1, M2's for every M2, and M4's for M3 and later; only the chip changes, so the
  error message keeps its version. Other APIs already tell the generations apart (WebGPU's atomic-vec2u-min-max from
  M2, AV1 hardware decoding in Media Capabilities and WebCodecs from M3), so a name from an earlier generation would
  contradict them. An M5 Pro goes from 9.7 bits to 7.5 (M4); an M1 reads 7.8 and an M2 8.1. The vendor string is
  already the same on every Apple GPU, and other GPUs (Linux, Windows, a virtual Mac's) keep their real name. Safari's
  "Apple GPU" scores 3.0 bits on its own, but next to Chrome's user agent and client hints it is a pairing only Ardia
  would send. When a user turns on ungoogled's SpoofWebGLInfo flag, its strings still win.

Both follow Helium's noise switches: the "Common CPU core count and GPU name" flag (Helium's hardware-concurrency
flag, reworded) turns both off, and a site exempt from Helium's noise reads the real values. The GPU itself, WebGL's
limits and Chrome's own thread pools are untouched: only a page that sizes a worker pool from hardwareConcurrency gets
fewer workers, on a machine with 3, 5 to 7, or more than 8 cores. Measured on an 18-core M5 Pro, interleaved against
the same engine without this patch: Speedometer 3.1 55.3 against 55.7 and JetStream 2.2 626 against 619 (noise), pools
of a fixed 8, 12 or 18 workers equal, and a CPU-bound pool sized from hardwareConcurrency 5.8 s against 3.6 s (8
workers against Helium's 14 or 16 in these runs; it picks 12 to 16). Shared by every platform: on Linux and Windows
the GPU name is unchanged and only the core count moves.

